- ## Prerequisites: llama-server (Auto-managed for Local URLs)
- HaS depends on llama-server to load the privacy model and provide inference. `has-text` now auto-reuses or auto-starts a local loopback `llama-server` when a command needs the model. Same-language `seek` avoids starting the server when deterministic restoration succeeds. **Only stop a server if this task started it. Never terminate a pre-existing server that you merely detected on the same port.**
- - Default server URL: `http://127.0.0.1:8080`
- - Override the auto-start model path with `HAS_TEXT_MODEL_PATH=/abs/path/to/has_text_model.gguf`
- 1. Pick the first URL to probe and export it, defaulting to 8080:
- ```bash
- export HAS_TEXT_SERVER="${HAS_TEXT_SERVER:-http://127.0.0.1:8080}"
- ```
- Use another free port instead if 8080 is already occupied by a different service.
- 2. Probe health: `curl -fsS "$HAS_TEXT_SERVER/health"`
- 3. If health is OK, identify the listening PID and inspect its command line:
- port="${HAS_TEXT_SERVER##*:}"
- pid="$(lsof -tiTCP:"$port" -sTCP:LISTEN | head -n 1)"
- 4. Reuse the existing service **only** if the command line clearly shows both `llama-server` and `has_text_model.gguf`. Health alone is not enough; another model may already be using that port.
- 5. If the running service is missing, unhealthy, or not the HaS model, leave that process alone and choose another free port (for example `8090`).
- 6. Decide the slot budget before starting llama-server. Use `1` for serial `hide`-only work; for chunk-parallel `scan`, model-backed `seek`, or batch `hide --dir`, set it to the number of model work items you expect, capped at `--max-parallel-requests` and 4 local slots. Keep `-c = 8192 * parallel` so each slot retains the full 8K context budget.
- 7. Start llama-server in the background and record the PID you started:
-   --port <port, default 8080> &
- 8. If using a non-8080 port, set an environment variable so the CLI knows: `export HAS_TEXT_SERVER=http://127.0.0.1:<port>`
- 9. Wait for readiness: poll the health endpoint until it returns ok
- | `--server URL` | llama-server address (env `HAS_TEXT_SERVER`). Loopback URLs are auto-managed; remote URLs are used as-is |
